"Internal Error ()\nciteturn18view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01443410.2022.2077915\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/full/10.1080/01443410.2022.2077915: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn18view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022096524000948\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0022096524000948: (403) Forbidden--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nUsing a Randomized Experiment to Compare Mastery Learning Thresholds (https://jedm.educationaldatamining.org/index.php/JEDM/article/download/900/253)\nciteturn18view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 29; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://jedm.educationaldatamining.org/index.php/JEDM/article/download/900/253\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1156\nL0@P0: Using a Randomized Experiment to Com\u0002pare Mastery Learning Thresholds\nL1@P0: Jeffrey Matayoshi\nL2@P0: McGraw Hill ALEKS\nL3@P0: Irvine, CA, USA\nL4@P0: jeffrey.matayoshi@mheducation.com\nL5@P0: Eric Cosyn\nL6@P0: McGraw Hill ALEKS\nL7@P0: Irvine, CA, USA\nL8@P0: eric.cosyn@mheducation.com\nL9@P0: Hasan Uzun\nL10@P0: McGraw Hill ALEKS\nL11@P0: Irvine, CA, USA\nL12@P0: hasan.uzun@mheducation.com\nL13@P0: Eyad Kurd-Misto\nL14@P0: McGraw Hill ALEKS\nL15@P0: Irvine, CA, USA\nL16@P0: eyad.kurd-misto@mheducation.com\nL17@P0: Many modern adaptive learning and intelligent tutoring systems implement the principles of mastery\nL18@P0: learning, where a student must demonstrate mastery of core prerequisite material before working on\nL19@P0: subsequent content within the system. Typically in such cases, a set of rules or algorithms is used to\nL20@P0: determine if a student has sufficiently mastered the concepts in a topic. In a previous work, we used a\nL21@P0: quasi-experimental design to investigate the relationship between two different mastery learning thresh\u0002olds and the forgetting of the learned material. As a follow-up to this initial study, in the present work,\nL22@P0: we analyze the results from a randomized experiment—or A/B test—directly comparing these two mas\u0002tery learning thresholds. These latest results seemingly agree with those from our initial study, giving\nL23@P0: evidence for the validity of the conclusions from our original quasi-experiment. In particular, we find\nL24@P0: that although students who learn with the higher mastery threshold are less likely to forget the learned\nL25@P0: knowledge, over time this difference decreases. Additionally, we build on these analyses by looking at\nL26@P0: how the relationships between the mastery thresholds change based on other factors, such as the amount\nL27@P0: of struggle students experience while learning or the subject matter being covered.\nL28@P0: Keywords: mastery learning, forgetting, intelligent tutoring system, randomized experiment\nL29@P0: 1. INTRODUCTION\nL30@P0: Within a mastery learning framework, students must demonstrate proficiency with the core pre\u0002requisite material before moving on to learn subsequent content. First articulated by Benjamin\nL31@P0: Bloom (Bloom, 1968), the principles of mastery learning are implemented in many modern\nL32@P0: adaptive learning and intelligent tutoring systems. Typically in these systems, a set of rules or\nL33@P0: algorithms determines if a student has sufficiently grasped the core material in a topic. Perhaps\nL34@P0: the most noteworthy of these systems is Bayesian knowledge tracing (BKT) and its many deriva\u0002tives (Baker et al., 2008; Corbett and Anderson, 1994; Pardos and Heffernan, 2011; Yudelson,\nL35@P0: 2016). Another common set of models is the factor analysis family—examples of which include\nL36@P0: 1\nL37@P0-1: 308 Journal of Educational Data Mining, Volume 17, No 1, 2025Learning Factors Analysis (LFA) (Cen et al., 2006) and Performance Factors Analysis (PFA)\nL38@P1: (Pavlik et al., 2009)—while simpler rules and heuristics, such as requiring students to correctly\nL39@P1: answer a certain number of questions in a row (Kelly et al., 2015), are also used.\nL40@P1: A closely related and relevant subject—both within the education field and more broadly\nL41@P1: as part of psychology and cognitive science—is that of knowledge retention and forgetting.\nL42@P1: Specifically, the Ebbinghaus forgetting curve (Averell and Heathcote, 2011; Ebbinghaus, 1913)\nL43@P1: is a well-known model of how knowledge decays over time. Many studies have examined these\nL44@P1: curves in a variety of settings, including laboratory experiments (Hanley-Dunn and McIntosh,\nL45@P1: 1984; McBride and Dosher, 1997; Paivio and Smythe, 1971; Smith, 1979), classrooms (Agarwal\nL46@P1: et al., 2012; Barzagar Nazari and Ebersbach, 2019; Goossens et al., 2016), and adaptive learning\nL47@P1: and intelligent tutoring systems (Matayoshi et al., 2018; Matayoshi et al., 2019; Wang and Beck,\nL48@P1: 2012; Xiong and Beck, 2014; Xiong et al., 2013). Furthermore, other studies have shown that\nL49@P1: accounting for forgetting (Choffin et al., 2019; Matayoshi et al., 2021; Qiu et al., 2011; Wang and\nL50@P1: Heffernan, 2011) and having personalized interventions and review schedules (Lindsey et al.,\nL51@P1: 2014; Pavlik and Anderson, 2008; Settles and Meeder, 2016; Tabibian et al., 2019; Xiong et al.,\nL52@P1: 2015) can be beneficial for learning systems.\nL53@P1: When implementing a mastery learning system, the chosen threshold must ensure that a\nL54@P1: student has sufficiently practiced a topic. However, there is a delicate balance at play, as stu\u0002dents can easily be subjected to more practice and work than necessary, an issue that has been\nL55@P1: variously referred to as “over practice” (Cen et al., 2007) or “overlearning” (Rohrer and Tay\u0002lor, 2006). With these issues in mind, previous works have looked in detail at mastery learning\nL56@P1: thresholds and how to optimize them for factors such as student learning efficiency (Balter et al., ¨\nL57@P1: 2018; Cen et al., 2007) and classification performance (Fancsali et al., 2013; Kelly et al., 2015).\nL58@P1: Additionally, it has been argued that the choice of data and the threshold used are more important\nL59@P1: than the specific type of model being applied (Pelanek and ´ Rih ˇ ak, 2017 ´ ).\nL60@P1: In the current study, we examine the relationship between different mastery thresholds and\nL61@P1: the long-term retention of the learned material; additionally, we analyze the frequencies at which\nL62@P1: students successfully learn topics using the different thresholds. This is a continuation of the\nL63@P1: work in Matayoshi et al. (2022), where we performed a quasi-experimental analysis comparing\nL64@P1: two different mastery thresholds used in the ALEKS adaptive learning system. In the current\nL65@P1: work, we further investigate the differences between the mastery thresholds by analyzing the\nL66@P1: results from a randomized experiment (or A/B test). This experiment has three main objectives.\nL67@P1: First, given the inherent limitations of quasi-experimental studies, we want to investigate if our\nL68@P1: previous results are consistent with those from a fully randomized experiment—such verification\nL69@P1: would give us more confidence in instituting changes to the way in which these thresholds are\nL70@P1: used within the ALEKS system. Second, such a result is of interest from a methodological\nL71@P1: standpoint, as it would validate the techniques used in the earlier observational study. Third, we\nL72@P1: would like to deepen our understanding by investigating how these relationships change based\nL73@P1: on other factors, such as the amount of struggle students experience while learning, the specific\nL74@P1: topic being worked on, or the subject area being studied. While the preliminary results of this\nL75@P1: experiment were first reported in Matayoshi et al. (2024), we have expanded on that initial work\nL76@P1: with a larger data set and additional analyses.\nL77@P1: The outline of the current paper is as follows. After reviewing several previous studies\nL78@P1: that are relevant to our current work, we continue by giving a brief background of the ALEKS\nL79@P1: system in Section 3. Next, in Section 4 we summarize the results from Matayoshi et al. (2022),\nL80@P1: and we then follow with a description of our experimental setup in Section 5. Our first analysis is\nL81@P1: 2\nL82@P1-2: 309 Journal of Educational Data Mining, Volume 17, No 1, 2025presented in Section 6, where we compare and contrast how often topics are successfully learned\nL83@P2: under the different mastery thresholds. Then, in Section 7 we study the retention and forgetting\nL84@P2: of learned knowledge where, among other things, we compare the experimental data with that\nL85@P2: from the original study in Matayoshi et al. (2022). In Section 8, we then look at how the results\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn18view3 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn17view0\",\"pattern\":\"116\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/09388982241292299: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn18view4 [wordlim: 200] Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn17view0\",\"pattern\":\"maintenance\"}); \nFailed to fetch https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/09388982241292299: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nArk Mathematics Mastery - first trial | EEF (https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/projects-and-evaluation/projects/mathematics-mastery-3)\nciteturn18view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn17view1\",\"pattern\":\"Key conclusions\"}); Total lines: 132\nNo matching text found for \"Key conclusions\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMastery learning | EEF (https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/education-evidence/teaching-learning-toolkit/mastery-learning)\nciteturn18view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn17view2\",\"pattern\":\"evidence\"}); Total lines: 140\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Evidence and resources】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Teaching and Learning Toolkit】\nL5: \nL6: 【3†Mastery learning】\nL7: \nL8: View in Cymraeg\nL9: # Mastery learning\nL10: \nL11: Moderate impact for very low cost based on limited evidence\nL12: \nL13: In mastery learning, learning outcomes are kept constant but the time needed for pupils to become proficient is varied.\nL14: \nL15: Implementation cost The cost estimates are based on the average cost of delivering the intervention.\nL16: \nL17: Evidence strength This rating provides an overall estimate of the robustness of the evidence, to help support professional decision-making in schools.\nL18: Impact (months)The impact measure shows the number of additional months of progress made, on average, by children and young people who received the intervention, compared to similar children and young people who did not.\nL19: \nL20: +5\nL21: \nL22: months\nL23: ## What is it?\nL24: \nL25: Mastery learning was originally developed in the 1960s. According to the early definition of mastery learning, learning outcomes are kept constant but the time needed for pupils to become proficient or competent at these objectives is varied.\nL26: Subject matter is broken into blocks or units with predetermined objectives and specified outcomes. Learners must demonstrate mastery on unit tests, typically 80%, before moving on to new material. Any pupils who do not achieve mastery are provided with extra support through a range of teaching strategies such as more intensive teaching, tutoring, peer-assisted learning, small group discussions, or additional homework. Learners continue the cycle of studying and testing until the mastery criteria are met.\nL27: More recent mastery approaches do not always have all these characteristics of mastery learning. Some approaches without a threshold typically involve the class moving on to new material when the teacher decides that the majority of pupils have mastered the unit. Curriculum time is varied according to the progress of the class. In other approaches, pupils are required to demonstrate mastery on a test to progress to new material, but there is not a specified threshold of at least 80%.\nL28: Mastery Learning should be distinguished from a related approach sometimes known as ​“teaching for mastery”. This term is often used to describe the approach to maths teaching found in high-performing places in East Asia, such as Shanghai and Singapore. Like ​“mastery learning”, ​“teaching for mastery” aims to support all pupils to achieve deep understanding and competence in the relevant topic.\nL29: However, ​“teaching for mastery” is characterised by teacher-led, whole-class teaching; common lesson content for all pupils; and use of manipulatives objects that educators and children can move and interact with to represent mathematical ideas (including fingers, everyday objects, such as buttons or pine cones, and mathematical resources such as Numicon, Cuisenaire rods). and representations.\nL30: Although some aspects of ​“teaching for mastery” are informed by research, relatively few interventions of this nature have been evaluated for impact. Most of the studies in this strand, should be distinguished from this related approach.\nL31: ## Key findings\nL32: \nL33: 1. Mastery learning is a cost-effective approach, on average, but is challenging to implement effectively. Schools should plan for changes and assess whether the approach is successful within their context.\nL34: \nL35: 2. A high level of success should be required before pupils move on to new content – it is crucial to monitor and communicate pupil progress and to provide additional support for pupils that take longer to reach the required level of knowledge.\nL36: 3. Mastery learning approaches are often associated with direct instruction, but many of the high impact studies identified included elements of collaborative learning.\nL37: \nL38: 4. There is large variation behind the average impact – mastery learning approaches have consistently positive impacts, but effects are higher for primary school pupils and in mathematics.\nL39: ## How effective is the approach?\nL40: \nL41: The impact of mastery learning approaches is an additional five months progress, on average, over the course of a year.\nL42: \nL43: There is a lot of variation behind this average. It seems to be important that a high bar is set for achievement of ​‘mastery’ (usually 80% to 90% on the relevant test). By contrast, the approach appears to be much less effective when pupils work at their own pace (see also 【4†Individualised instruction】).\nL44: Mastery learning also appears to be particularly effective when pupils are given opportunities to work in groups or teams and take responsibility for supporting each other’s progress (see also 【5†Collaborative learning】 and 【6†Peer tutoring】).\nL45: ## Behind the average\nL46: \nL47:   * Studies involving primary school pupils have tended to be more effective (+8 months) than for secondary school pupils (+ 3 months).\nL48: \nL49:   * Mastery learning has been used successfully across the curriculum but particularly for reading, mathematics and science. Effects are higher in mathematics and science (+6 months) than reading (+3 months).\nL50: \nL51:   * A high level of mastery of about 80% is associated with more successful approaches.\nL52:   * Mastery learning approaches that include collaborative learning can be effective.\nL53: ## Closing the disadvantage gap\nL54: \nL55: Mastery learning approaches aim to ensure that all pupils have mastered key concepts before moving on to the next topic – in contrast with traditional teaching methods in which pupils may be left behind, with gaps of misunderstanding widening. Mastery learning approaches could address these challenges by giving additional time and support to pupils who may have missed learning, or take longer to master new knowledge and skills.\nL56: In order for mastery approaches to be effective for pupils with gaps in understanding, it is crucial that additional support is provided. Approaches that simply build upon foundational knowledge without targeting support for pupils that fall behind are unlikely to narrow disadvantage gaps.\nL57: ## How could you implement in your setting?\nL58: \nL59: Mastery learning works through designing units of work so that each task has a clear learning outcome, which pupils must master prior to moving on to the next task. Core components of the mastery approach that schools should be careful to implement include:\nL60:   * Effective diagnostic assessment to identify areas of strength and weakness\nL61:   * Carefully sequencing topics so that they gradually build on foundational knowledge\nL62:   * Flexibility for teachers on how long they need to spend on any particular topic\nL63:   * Monitoring of pupil learning and regular feedback so that pupils can master topics prior to moving to the next\nL64:   * Additional support for pupils that struggle to master topic areas\nL65: Mastery learning interventions are typically delivered over the course of an academic year, as choosing to take longer on topic or scheme of work requires flexibility in the planning and teaching of curriculum content.\nL66: \nL67: Some schools may decide that certain topics are more suited to a mastery approach than others, and therefore the delivery time could be as short as half a term.\nL68: When introducing new approaches, schools should consider implementation. For more information see 【7†Putting Evidence to Work – A School’s Guide to Implementation.】\nL69: ## What does it cost\nL70: \nL71: Overall, the median costs of implementing mastery learning approaches are estimated as very low. The costs associated with mastery learning approaches mostly arise from professional development training for teaching staff, which is most commonly a start-up cost for introducing the new approach.\nL72: Whilst the average cost estimate for mastery learning is very low, the range in costs of professional development training, and the option to pay for ongoing training and additional staff to provider greater timetable flexibility, mean that costs can range from very low to moderate.\nL73: Implementing mastery learning will also require a moderate amount of staff time, compared with other approaches. School leaders should be aware of the extra staff time required and think carefully about other activities they might need to cut back on to provide this additional support.\nL74: \nL75: Alongside time and cost, school leaders should consider how to maximise support for struggling learners and avoid some pupils getting bored or frustrated whilst they wait for others to master content.\nL76: ## How secure is the evidence?\nL77: \nL78: The security of the evidence around mastery learning is rated as low. 80 studies were identified that meet the inclusion criteria of the Toolkit. Overall, the topic lost two additional padlocks because:\nL79:   * A small percentage of studies that have taken place recently. This might mean that the research is not representative of current practice.\nL80:   * A large percentage of the studies are not randomised controlled trials. While other study designs still give important information about effectiveness of approaches, there is a risk that results are influenced by unknown factors that are not part of the intervention.\nL81: As with any evidence review, the Toolkit summarises the average impact of approaches when researched in academic studies. It is important to consider your context and apply your professional judgement when implementing an approach in your setting.\nL82: \nL83: Evidence strength This rating provides an overall estimate of the robustness of the evidence, to help support professional decision-making in schools.\nL84: \nL85: Number of studies 80\nL86: \nL87: Review last updated September 2021\nL88: ### Have any questions?\nL89: \nL90: The Teaching and Learning Toolkit is a living resource that is updated as new evidence is published. As we continue to update the Toolkit we are interested in your questions about this topic area for future content.\nL91: \nL92: The evidence synthesis team will review submitted questions and, where possible, provide answers in future updates.\nL93: \nL94: ## Relevant EEF studies\nL95: \nL96: ## Learn more\nL97: \nL98: 【8†Image: Cards gr maths ks2 ks3†d2rty5wuu5bi5t.cloudfront.net】\nL99: \nL100: Guidance Reports\nL101: ### Improving Mathematics in Key Stages 2 and 3\nL102: \nL103: Improve maths teaching for seven – 14 year olds.\nL104: \nL105: Primary Secondary\nL106: \nL107: 【9†Read more about Improving Mathematics in Key Stages 2 and 3】\nL108: \nL109: 【10†Image: Cards gr early years maths†d2rty5wuu5bi5t.cloudfront.net】\nL110: \nL111: Guidance Reports\nL112: \nL113: ### Improving Mathematics in the Early Years and Key Stage 1\nL114: \nL115: Support maths understanding for three – seven year olds.\nL116: \nL117: Early Years Primary\nL118: \nL119: 【11†Read more about Improving Mathematics in the Early Years and Key Stage 1】\nL120: ## Quick links\nL121: \nL122:   * 【12†Careers at the EEF】\nL123:   * 【13†Get in touch】\nL124:   * 【14†Evaluation resources】\nL125:   * 【15†Evidence and practice reviews】\nL126:   * 【16†Open tenders】\nL127:   * 【17†Newsletters】\nL128:   * 【18†News and blog】\nL129:   * 【19†Privacy notices】\nL130:   * 【20†Accessibility】\nL131: ## Keep-up-to date with our latest news and resources\nL132: \nL133: Our newsletters are e‑mailed to 60,000+ subscribers regularly.\nL134: \nL135: 【17†Register for updates】\nL136: \nL137: Page generated on: Thursday, 5 November 2026 at 14:18 (E)\nL138: \nL139: The Education Endowment Foundation (EEF) is a charity and a company limited by guarantee. Registered in England, Number 114 2111 © 2026, Education Endowment Foundation, all rights reserved.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMastery learning | EEF (https://educationendowmentfoundation.org.uk/education-evidence/teaching-learning-toolkit/mastery-learning)\nciteturn18view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn17view2\",\"pattern\":\"time\"}); Total lines: 140\nL0: 【0†iframe†www.googletagmanager.com】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Evidence and resources】\nL3: \nL4: 【2†Teaching and Learning Toolkit】\nL5: \nL6: 【3†Mastery learning】\nL7: \nL8: View in Cymraeg\nL9: # Mastery learning\nL10: \nL11: Moderate impact for very low cost based on limited evidence\nL12: \nL13: In mastery learning, learning outcomes are kept constant but the time needed for pupils to become proficient is varied.\nL14: \nL15: Implementation cost The cost estimates are based on the average cost of delivering the intervention.\nL16: \nL17: Evidence strength This rating provides an overall estimate of the robustness of the evidence, to help support professional decision-making in schools.\nL18: Impact (months)The impact measure shows the number of additional months of progress made, on average, by children and young people who received the intervention, compared to similar children and young people who did not.\nL19: \nL20: +5\nL21: \nL22: months\nL23: ## What is it?\nL24: \nL25: Mastery learning was originally developed in the 1960s. According to the early definit