pupils (+ 3 months).\nL48: \nL49:   * Mastery learning has been used successfully across the curriculum but particularly for reading, mathematics and science. Effects are higher in mathematics and science (+6 months) than reading (+3 months).\nL50: \nL51:   * A high level of mastery of about 80% is associated with more successful approaches.\nL52:   * Mastery learning approaches that include collaborative learning can be effective.\nL53: ## Closing the disadvantage gap\nL54: \nL55: Mastery learning approaches aim to ensure that all pupils have mastered key concepts before moving on to the next topic – in contrast with traditional teaching methods in which pupils may be left behind, with gaps of misunderstanding widening. Mastery learning approaches could address these challenges by giving additional time and support to pupils who may have missed learning, or take longer to master new knowledge and skills.\nL56: In order for mastery approaches to be effective for pupils with gaps in understanding, it is crucial that additional support is provided. Approaches that simply build upon foundational knowledge without targeting support for pupils that fall behind are unlikely to narrow disadvantage gaps.\nL57: ## How could you implement in your setting?\nL58: \nL59: Mastery learning works through designing units of work so that each task has a clear learning outcome, which pupils must master prior to moving on to the next task. Core components of the mastery approach that schools should be careful to implement include:\nL60:   * Effective diagnostic assessment to identify areas of strength and weakness\nL61:   * Carefully sequencing topics so that they gradually build on foundational knowledge\nL62:   * Flexibility for teachers on how long they need to spend on any particular topic\nL63:   * Monitoring of pupil learning and regular feedback so that pupils can master topics prior to moving to the next\nL64:   * Additional support for pupils that struggle to master topic areas\nL65: Mastery learning interventions are typically delivered over the course of an academic year, as choosing to take longer on topic or scheme of work requires flexibility in the planning and teaching of curriculum content.\nL66: \nL67: Some schools may decide that certain topics are more suited to a mastery approach than others, and therefore the delivery time could be as short as half a term.\nL68: When introducing new approaches, schools should consider implementation. For more information see 【7†Putting Evidence to Work – A School’s Guide to Implementation.】\nL69: ## What does it cost\nL70: \nL71: Overall, the median costs of implementing mastery learning approaches are estimated as very low. The costs associated with mastery learning approaches mostly arise from professional development training for teaching staff, which is most commonly a start-up cost for introducing the new approach.\nL72: Whilst the average cost estimate for mastery learning is very low, the range in costs of professional development training, and the option to pay for ongoing training and additional staff to provider greater timetable flexibility, mean that costs can range from very low to moderate.\nL73: Implementing mastery learning will also require a moderate amount of staff time, compared with other approaches. School leaders should be aware of the extra staff time required and think carefully about other activities they might need to cut back on to provide this additional support.\nL74: \nL75: Alongside time and cost, school leaders should consider how to maximise support for struggling learners and avoid some pupils getting bored or frustrated whilst they wait for others to master content.\nL76: ## How secure is the evidence?\nL77: \nL78: The security of the evidence around mastery learning is rated as low. 80 studies were identified that meet the inclusion criteria of the Toolkit. Overall, the topic lost two additional padlocks because:\nL79:   * A small percentage of studies that have taken place recently. This might mean that the research is not representative of current practice.\nL80:   * A large percentage of the studies are not randomised controlled trials. While other study designs still give important information about effectiveness of approaches, there is a risk that results are influenced by unknown factors that are not part of the intervention.\nL81: As with any evidence review, the Toolkit summarises the average impact of approaches when researched in academic studies. It is important to consider your context and apply your professional judgement when implementing an approach in your setting.\nL82: \nL83: Evidence strength This rating provides an overall estimate of the robustness of the evidence, to help support professional decision-making in schools.\nL84: \nL85: Number of studies 80\nL86: \nL87: Review last updated September 2021\nL88: ### Have any questions?\nL89: \nL90: The Teaching and Learning Toolkit is a living resource that is updated as new evidence is published. As we continue to update the Toolkit we are interested in your questions about this topic area for future content.\nL91: \nL92: The evidence synthesis team will review submitted questions and, where possible, provide answers in future updates.\nL93: \nL94: ## Relevant EEF studies\nL95: \nL96: ## Learn more\nL97: \nL98: 【8†Image: Cards gr maths ks2 ks3†d2rty5wuu5bi5t.cloudfront.net】\nL99: \nL100: Guidance Reports\nL101: ### Improving Mathematics in Key Stages 2 and 3\nL102: \nL103: Improve maths teaching for seven – 14 year olds.\nL104: \nL105: Primary Secondary\nL106: \nL107: 【9†Read more about Improving Mathematics in Key Stages 2 and 3】\nL108: \nL109: 【10†Image: Cards gr early years maths†d2rty5wuu5bi5t.cloudfront.net】\nL110: \nL111: Guidance Reports\nL112: \nL113: ### Improving Mathematics in the Early Years and Key Stage 1\nL114: \nL115: Support maths understanding for three – seven year olds.\nL116: \nL117: Early Years Primary\nL118: \nL119: 【11†Read m